title: The Cincinnati Banner Holds
subtitle: The Liberal Republicans somehow march on
tags: event
view-if: liberal_reunion_triggered = 0 and liberal_split = 1 and year = 1873 and month = 2

= Willard Hotel Rally

After the 1872 election, the Liberal Republican Party's lead members, seeing the potential of their party to contest the Republicans and fight against corruption, big government, etc, have decided to keep the party operational.

It still remains how long this liberal experiment will last for. Delegates gather at the Willard Hotel, not to mourn Greeley's defeat but to argue about what comes next. Carl Schurz insists the party's principles, civil service reform, hard money, and an end to military Reconstruction, are more urgent now than they were in 1872, and that abandoning the organization would hand the field back to the same corruption they set out to fight. Charles Francis Adams, characteristically aloof, warns that a party without a President, without patronage, and without a newspaper as loud as Greeley's *Tribune* is a party running on fumes.

Others in the room are already looking past the Republicans entirely. Quiet conversations turn toward the <span style="color: #0015bc;">**Democratic Party**</span>, and whether men like Samuel Tilden, who share the Liberals' contempt for Republican-era corruption without sharing the Bourbon South's appetite for reversing Reconstruction outright, might eventually make better partners than either of the parties that already exist. No one says so out loud. Not yet.

By the end of the meeting, the national committee votes narrowly to keep the party's organization intact, its officers in place, and its press allies writing. It is a decision to survive, not a plan for how. The next real test will come with the 1874 midterms, when the country will learn whether the Liberal Republican experiment is a movement with a future or a footnote to Grant's second term.

- @damn: Shame...

@damn

The gavel falls, and the delegates file out into the Washington cold, still a party, for now.